### THE ANALYSER SUBSYSTEM — nine small analysers over one snapshot
###
### Every request here is a real one against a running instance. Sign in first: the session cookie and
### the cross-site token are what the chain expects. The demo backend runs on 8081 — Tomcat9 owns 8080
### on this machine.
###
### **Four endpoints, not nine, and that is deliberate.** Two to run and diagnose, two to work the
### queue. The engineering boundary must not become the
### navigation boundary: nine analysers produce findings and a person meets one run. An endpoint per
### analyser would make the subsystem's internal structure the thing somebody has to learn, and "which
### of the nine told me this" is a question nobody has.
###
### **Why nine small ones rather than one pipeline.** The node pipeline was built as a single thing
### running from raw nodes to discovered processes. Its first real run reported zero, and it took four
### separate investigations to find out why: no finished engagements in the window, marked text that
### was chatter, `output_type` written to the bracket while the pipeline read the node, and a
### truncation warning that crashed at exactly 200 nodes. Four independent causes, one output, each
### silently emptying the next — so everything upstream looked correct and produced nothing.
###
### **The figure to watch is `itemsRead`, and it is not decoration.** No findings across 400 brackets is
### a real answer about a business. The same silence across 0 brackets means the thing is not running.
### Without the count those are the same response.
###
### Requires PIPELINE_RUN_START — the same permission the node pipeline's run control uses, because it
### is the same act: asking the installation to look at its own graph.

@host = http://localhost:8081
@employee = ionut@atelier.ro
@password = password1234
### Paste one from `groups[].items[].id` in GET /api/analysis/findings before sending the dismissal.
@findingId = 00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000

###############################################################################################
### ANALYSER-RUN-01 — look at the graph, and let every analyser answer
###############################################################################################

### Run every analyser over one snapshot. 200.
###
### **One run, one window, one snapshot, many analysers.** Every analyser reads the same object, so two
### of them cannot disagree from different eras — the defect class that has cost this project five
### sessions. Read `windowFrom` and `windowTo` off the response: they are always there, because an
### analyser answering honestly about very little is indistinguishable from a broken one unless the
### span it looked at is beside the answer.
POST {{host}}/api/analysis/runs

### The same, with the window stated.
###
### **The default is 90 days rather than 30, and the number is measured.** This corpus is 76%
### future-dated: a 30-day window holds 83 of 348 nodes. Run this with `windowDays=30` and then with
### the default, and watch `itemsRead` move — that difference is the reason the default is not 30.
POST {{host}}/api/analysis/runs?windowDays=30

### 400. A window of zero days holds nothing to analyse, and says so rather than running over nothing
### and reporting a confident silence.
POST {{host}}/api/analysis/runs?windowDays=0

###############################################################################################
### ANALYSER-RUN-01 — what the last run said, analyser by analyser
###############################################################################################

### Four things per analyser, and the fourth is the one that matters: what it read, what it found,
### what it needed, and **what it could not see**.
###
### That last column is the diagnostic that would have replaced four investigations with one screen.
### Look for:
###
###   `itemsRead`      — silence over 400 and silence over 0 are different facts
###   `absences`       — "212 of 348 nodes have no work type". `blocking` says whether it stopped the
###                      analyser or merely narrowed it, and without that distinction the panel
###                      becomes noise and stops being read
###   `clean`          — "18 approved templates checked; every one has matched work." **NOT an
###                      absence.** An absence is "I could not see"; this is "I saw, and it is fine".
###                      Three analysers in the simulation emitted neither, which on screen reads as
###                      broken — so the runner now REFUSES a report carrying no finding, no absence
###                      and no clean result, and marks the run failed naming who was silent
###   `preconditions`  — "needs at least 3 finished engagements; you have 0". **An analyser never
###                      returns a silent zero**: the unmet precondition IS the output
###   `failure`        — that analyser's own exception. One broken analyser does not take the other
###                      eight down, and a run that lost one says so instead of looking complete
GET {{host}}/api/analysis/runs/latest

### 204, before anything has ever been run.
###
### Not an empty run object — those are different facts and the caller is owed the difference. Reset
### the database and send this before any POST above to see it.
GET {{host}}/api/analysis/runs/latest

###############################################################################################
### The Owner page — a queue, not a report
###############################################################################################

### ANALYSER-VIEW-FINDINGS-01 · Requires PIPELINE_RUN_START
###
### A report is read once; a queue is worked, and that distinction decides the whole shape.
###
### `groups`   — the five categories, always in the same order, so the sections do not move between
###              visits. Sorted worst-first INSIDE each group and never across it (A14): reach counts
###              templates for one analyser and brackets for another, so one global ordering weighs
###              41 of one against 153 of the other and is arbitrary while looking authoritative
### `priority` — comparable only within its group. Nobody is shown the number
### `whyItRanks` — the number's reason, in one line: "High because it touches 41 of 63 and it is
###              worse than last run." An opaque rank is the first thing an owner dismisses, and
###              then the second
### `timesSeen` — a finding shown past its grace and not acted on fades, floored so it never
###              disappears, and says "Shown 6 times, unchanged." Worse than when you last looked
###              resets that, because a finding that has just got worse must not arrive already faded
### `standing` — including `nothingNew`. Eight of sixteen simulated weeks had nothing new; that is
###              correct behaviour and a presentation problem, because a page that renders an
###              unchanged list without saying so is indistinguishable from one that failed to load
###
### 204 where no analysis has ever been run.
GET {{host}}/api/analysis/findings

### ANALYSER-DISMISS-FINDING-01 · Requires PIPELINE_RUN_START
###
### "Not now" — and it survives the next run, which is the whole point. The analysers re-derive the
### same finding from every snapshot, so without the record it would be back the next morning, and a
### queue that re-asks a question you have already answered is a queue people stop opening.
###
### It comes back on exactly two conditions, neither of them a timer (A3): it reaches HALF AGAIN as
### far as it did when you said no, or it stops being the same kind of problem — a MEDIUM suggestion
### that has become CRITICAL is not the question anybody answered. It returns marked WORSENING rather
### than NEW, so the page can say you dismissed this at 41 and it is 62 now.
###
### Half again is measured AGAINST THE DISMISSAL, never against the previous run. A finding creeping
### up a fifth per run never grows by half in one step, so the other reading would hold the dismissal
### forever while the finding nearly doubled — A3 defeated by arithmetic rather than by anybody
### deciding.
###
### The identifier is the finding row on the run you were looking at, so what is dismissed is the
### sighting you actually saw. Take it from `groups[].items[].id` above.
###
### 204 on success · 404 where the identifier names no finding of any run.
POST {{host}}/api/analysis/findings/{{findingId}}/dismiss

###############################################################################################
### What is deliberately absent
###############################################################################################

### **The judgement is never written into `analysis_finding`.** What a run found is a fact about the
### graph, and a person disagreeing about whether to act on it does not make the finding untrue. The
### decision goes to `insight_decision`, which V51 built for exactly this and is append-only — so a
### re-run cannot overwrite a judgement, and a finding dismissed, revived by growth and dismissed
### again keeps all three rows.
###
### **No endpoint un-dismisses.** Nothing here is updated or deleted; the newest row about a finding
### is its current state.
###
### **No finding is ranked across analysers.** `reach` counts nodes for one and templates for another,
### so a single global sort would put 41 templates above 153 brackets on no principle at all. The owner
### page groups by category first and sorts by reach inside the group.
###
### **A cumulative count is judged on its share.** Findings carry `reachOf`; where it is present the
### lifecycle compares the ratio against five percentage points rather than the count against a
### multiplier. Measured over sixteen simulated weeks, a count-only rule held one finding at the top of
### the queue for ten of them while the underlying rate was improving.
###
### **No endpoint takes a confidence threshold.** Sweeping the thresholds changed zero decisions —
### every real decision comes from a boolean gate — and a slider would imply control that does not
### exist.
###
### **No endpoint runs one analyser.** Two analysers reading at different moments can disagree from
### different eras, and an endpoint that ran one of them would be the supported way to produce exactly
### that.
###
### **AI is off, and `flowops.ai.*` is consumed by nothing yet.** Seven of the nine analysers need none
### at all. The configuration surface exists so that Phase 10 is a matter of reading it rather than of
### touching every analyser — two switches, per analyser, because a global toggle cannot attribute a
### regression to the one thing that changed.

### ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
### What one finding rests on — every reference resolved
### ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
###
### Five subject kinds, not three. `nodes` and `templates` and `jobs` were the whole answer until
### 2026-09-03, while EffortAnalyser and WaitingAnalyser had been writing BRACKET and WAIT evidence
### all along — so opening `median_time_to_finish` or `wait_never_satisfied` returned three empty
### lists, which this endpoint's own description says means the finding rests on engagements.
###
### `brackets[]` carries the close kind and the elapsed minutes, because only DELIVERED and DONE are
### work finishing and a reader checking a duration needs to see which of the nine it was.
### `waits[]` carries the kind, because CLIENT and SUPPLIER are outside the workspace's control and
### never enter a performance figure (R7.5) — the same number means opposite things either side of it.
###
### 404 rather than five empty lists when the id names nothing.
GET {{host}}/api/analysis/findings/{{findingId}}/evidence
